# ============================================================
#  Alignment (design §8.2): Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−).
#  Thresholds ±30 and ±60 open these events; characters tagged
#  `virtuous` or `ruthless` (content/world/cast.grim) react to
#  where you stand. Deep in the Hollow the way back is slow
#  (the resource's drag), so the way-back events cost real effort.
#  Hollow Star plot scenes that read alignment live in
#  chain_hollowstar.grim (hs_grey_invitation, hs_grey_tests,
#  hs_wardens_notice).
# ============================================================

# ---------------- Hollow (alignment <= -30) ----------------

@event align_cold_candles
title: The Candles Lean Away
class: routine
tone: eerie
when: alignment <= -30
repeat: 12
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: hollow
scene: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.
text: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}
> left: Let them lean
  guile +small
  alignment -small
  renown -tiny
  say: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.
> right: Stop and relight one by hand
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.

@event align_whispered_answer
title: A Voice Over Your Shoulder
class: routine
tone: eerie
when: alignment <= -30 and term
repeat: 12
weight: 13
art: eye
tags: hollow study
scene: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.
text: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.
> left: Write it down
  lore +small
  alignment -small
  say: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.
> right: Close the book
  mood -small
  alignment +small
  say: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.

@event align_maud_way_back
title: Weeding, Dearie
class: routine
tone: warm
actor: maud
trigger: social
when: alignment <= -30 and met(maud)
repeat: 8
weight: 14
art: leaf
tags: hollow nature
scene: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.
text: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. "You've a cold look about you, dearie," she says, and holds out a trowel. "Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest."
> left: Kneel and start pulling
  health -small
  alignment +medium
  rel maud +small
  say: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.
> right: "I'm fine."
  rel maud -small
  mood +tiny
  say: "Course you are," Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.

@event align_reproach
title: "I Don't Know You Lately"
class: routine
tone: tense
cast:
  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 6
per: friend
trigger: social
when: alignment <= -30
repeat: 12
weight: 12
art: heartbreak
tags: social hollow
scene: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.
text: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. "People talk," {they} say{s} at last. "I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right."
> left: "You are. I'll do better."
  alignment +small
  mood -small
  rel friend +small
  say: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.
> right: "Mind your own business."
  rel friend -medium
  alignment -tiny
  guile +tiny
  say: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.

@event align_ruthless_nod
title: Finally, Someone Who Gets It
class: routine
tone: tense
cast:
  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless)
per: other
trigger: social
when: alignment <= -30
repeat: 12
weight: 10
art: mask
tags: social hollow
scene: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.
text: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.
> left: Nod back
  rel other +medium
  alignment -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.
> right: Keep walking
  rel other -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.

@event align_northern_parcel
title: A Parcel for the North
class: routine
tone: tense
when: alignment <= -30
repeat: 12
weight: 13
art: pouch
tags: hollow money
scene: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.
text: {~A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} "For the boathouse, after dark," {~they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. "The families remember who is reliable."} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.
> left: Carry it
  gold +medium
  alignment -medium
  health -tiny
  say: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.
> right: Walk away
  mood -tiny
  alignment +tiny
  say: You leave the courier holding it. "Pity," they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.

@event align_cruel_advantage
title: Something You Could Use
class: routine
tone: tense
cast:
  mark: any where tagged(mark, student) and rel(mark) < 12
per: mark
when: alignment <= -30
repeat: 12
weight: 13
art: scroll
tags: social hollow
scene: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.
text: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.
> left: Keep it
  guile +small
  renown +tiny
  alignment -small
  rel mark -small
  say: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.
> right: Hand it back
  rel mark +small
  mood -tiny
  alignment +small
  say: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says "thank you" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.

@event align_frost_in_the_spell
title: The Cold That Helps
class: routine
tone: eerie
when: alignment <= -30 and term
repeat: 12
weight: 13
art: snow
tags: hollow study
scene: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.
text: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.
> left: Let the cold in
  arcana +small
  alignment -small
  health -tiny
  say: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.
> right: Practise the slow way
  arcana +tiny
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.

# ---------------- Lumen (alignment >= 30) ----------------

@event align_asked_for_help
title: "You're the One People Ask"
class: routine
tone: warm
when: alignment >= 30
repeat: 12
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: social lumen
scene: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.
text: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under "ask them".} "Somebody said you'd know what to do," {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.}
> left: Sit them down and help
  health -small
  renown +small
  alignment +tiny
  say: It takes the whole evening. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.
> right: Point them to a tutor
  mood +tiny
  alignment -tiny
  say: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.

@event align_warm_regard
title: A Good Word
class: routine
tone: warm
cast:
  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 3
per: friend
trigger: social
when: alignment >= 30
repeat: 12
weight: 10
art: heart
tags: social lumen
scene: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.
text: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. "I heard what you did," {they} say{s}. "Don't make a face. It was good. Someone should say so."
> left: Accept the biscuit, and the praise
  rel friend +small
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.
> right: Change the subject
  rel friend +tiny
  mood -tiny
  alignment +tiny
  say: You talk about the weather. {friend} lets you, but the look {they} give{s} you says the subject has only been postponed.

@event align_saint_jibe
title: "Saint"
class: routine
tone: tense
cast:
  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless) and tagged(other, peer)
per: other
trigger: social
when: alignment >= 30
repeat: 12
weight: 10
art: scales
tags: social lumen
scene: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.
text: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. "Here comes the saint," {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. "Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?"
> left: Let it go
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.
> right: Give as good as you get
  rel other -small
  mood +small
  guile +tiny
  say: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.
